        "problem": "Solve the inequality ${(0.72)}^{x}-4\\le 0.$",
        "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.72)}^{x}-4 & \\le 0 \\\\ {(0.72)}^{x} & \\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061[(0.72)^{x}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.72) & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.72)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0.72)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
